WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.140
Arch Rivals, it's not just basketball, it's a basket brawl!

00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:29.580
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:31.880
I'm Mike. And I'm Sean.

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:34.320
It's a basket brawl.

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:36.980
Sean, they coined the term.

00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:38.180
No one's ever heard that.

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:40.420
I'm sure, yeah, no one's ever said that before.

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:44.220
I mean, maybe, is this like basketball?

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:54.280
Is basketball actually like anywhere near the level of cleverness as basket brawl, I ask

00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:57.420
you? I guess it really depends on who you ask.

00:00:57.680 --> 00:00:59.200
And you could do this with everything, right?

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:01.580
Base brawl, foot brawl.

00:01:01.580 --> 00:01:05.200
Like, where's the, where's like the VHS copy of foot brawl?

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:09.080
That's just like fights breaking out in football games in like the 70s and 80s.

00:01:09.220 --> 00:01:10.400
Someone has to own that.

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:16.440
Yeah, they're probably a bunch of like, they're actually just like the old school kind of like

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:17.460
VHS remix.

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:22.520
Like they're not, they're just some guy like timing the record button on his VCR.

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:29.420
Yeah. And it's funny that like, I feel like that stuff, like as clips has made it to YouTube,

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:34.240
but we need that's like, we need that full program like uploaded, right?

00:01:34.340 --> 00:01:37.920
There used to be like a collection of like baseball bloopers, right?

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:39.120
Yeah. Hockey fights, right?

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:41.900
It's like, just upload the whole 60 minutes, guys.

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:44.000
Like we don't need to break this thing up.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:45.260
It was enjoyable back then.

00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:46.220
It's enjoyable now.

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:53.000
And I guess, you know, for arch rivals sake, it's trying to at least separate itself from

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:57.880
the other basketball games of which there are a shocking amount of on the NES because

00:01:57.880 --> 00:02:02.860
most of them are already doing like, okay, this is, this is five on five.

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:04.240
This is two V two.

00:02:04.420 --> 00:02:05.460
This is street basketball.

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:07.480
Like everybody's already kind of carving out a niche.

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:12.300
So arch rivals, which is a interesting name, probably should have just been called basketball.

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:18.100
Uh, but arch rivals, maybe there's a joke about like, you know, you, how you shoot at

00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:19.800
the, at the tip of the arch or whatever.

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:23.980
No. Okay. I think it's, I don't think it means anything.

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:24.820
I think you're right.

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:25.720
That is terrible.

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:27.500
It's a terrible name. Yeah.

00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:33.300
So it's a two on two basketball, arcade basketball by Midway.

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:39.680
Uh, first in the arcades in 1989, the NES port comes to us in November of 1990, which means

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:41.200
we're almost done with 1990, Sean.

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:46.980
Yeah. I'm still kind of counting down the days until I can say I was finally born during this movie.

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:48.280
I'm sorry. During this game.

00:02:48.740 --> 00:02:50.540
Yeah. This has just been a movie guys.

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:53.060
Everybody's been following the plot of the show, right?

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:59.380
Uh, but no, we, we, we, we keep, we keep inching closer and never, never quite getting there.

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:06.640
And while the game is known for, um, making, uh, fighting and hitting and slapping, uh, legal,

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:13.500
uh, I guess it's also like, it matters a bit because it's the prototype for Midway's, uh,

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:19.120
you know, soon to be arcade game in 1993 and Charles Barkley shut up and jam.

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:21.900
Oh yeah. There's jam in the title.

00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:27.840
So I understand how you got confused, but it's not Charles Barkley shut up and jam or Charles

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:31.980
Barkley shut up and jam Gaten, uh, which is everybody should check that out.

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:40.700
Yeah. What I was referencing, um, the, yeah, it's very, uh, not obvious, but like, it's very close

00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:43.220
to what would become jam.

00:03:43.220 --> 00:03:50.280
I mean, I think like, it's kind of crazy that this game is basically like NFL blitz or NHL

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:51.720
hits or yada yada.

00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:56.080
And then they kind of toned it down for NBA jam.

00:03:56.080 --> 00:04:03.480
Uh, I would have thought usually it's like these things get more intense as, uh, as time

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:08.580
goes on. Um, but yeah, you can definitely feel the, the lineage in here.

00:04:09.100 --> 00:04:15.340
Yeah. That, that is a good point that, uh, especially once blitz hit the market that like everyone

00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:20.600
followed that with hits and slug fest and, you know, it got way more extreme than you have

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:21.440
the street games.

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:29.700
Uh, but I feel like even here, uh, it's, uh, you know, like, like the hitting and the bumping

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:30.880
into the ref and everything.

00:04:30.920 --> 00:04:36.980
Like it is a, it is an interesting approach at like comedy as a whole, right?

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:40.400
Like you're not hitting like street fighter hard, right?

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:42.180
It's not like a beat them up style thing.

00:04:42.260 --> 00:04:45.780
It's like, you know, you're just kind of like, you're poking this guy and then you got the

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:49.240
ball and he's on the ground and then you try to get out through, but then the ref's in

00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:51.500
the way. And he's tiny.

00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:58.940
Right. It's just like, it, it, it's a little like silly, but in a way that like makes you again,

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:02.880
like that comedy thing. And like, it doesn't make you like laugh out loud or anything, but I don't know.

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:04.600
Like there was a lot of charm in this one.

00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:08.340
Absolutely. It's kind of all just a big pratfall routine.

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:17.320
I mean, and like the, I wouldn't call them cut scenes, but like they maybe overdo that,

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:24.240
but like each, each time you score a bucket of any kind, you get some weird image and like

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:31.880
just the, the way that the ball goes in the net can be in that same like goofball vein.

00:05:31.880 --> 00:05:42.980
Like it just does the like really extravagant bounce outs or a really quick like rotation

00:05:42.980 --> 00:05:44.040
around the rim.

00:05:44.260 --> 00:05:49.120
And then this proceeds jam and how you can break the glass.

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:55.880
So all of it is kind of serving that, like, we're not taking this sport all that seriously.

00:05:56.520 --> 00:06:00.900
Yeah. And there are, there are four quarters to the game.

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:04.020
The NES version defaults to three minutes for each quarter.

00:06:04.420 --> 00:06:06.200
If you're tied after that, you go to overtime.

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:11.720
What is the ideal like amount of time in it?

00:06:11.900 --> 00:06:16.540
Not even just in basketball games, but just in like, like, like time in your head of how

00:06:16.540 --> 00:06:24.420
long a quarter or a, um, you know, uh, a half or whatever in any of these games should be.

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:28.460
Is it like, is three minutes of real time a good amount?

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:29.520
Is that too long?

00:06:29.660 --> 00:06:34.500
Should this be like so fast that you could pick up and play a full game in five minutes

00:06:34.500 --> 00:06:35.900
and then you just run it back?

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:37.140
Like what, what's your thoughts there?

00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:40.540
Um, well, I think it's four minutes.

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:41.840
Uh, okay.

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:47.060
So I think that's actually pretty good for like the pace of this game.

00:06:47.500 --> 00:06:51.260
It is pretty fast and there is a lot of stoppages.

00:06:51.580 --> 00:06:58.560
Like when, again, it has to cut to a two second image, uh, every time you score.

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:01.800
So that time is also kind of getting inflated.

00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:07.760
You might, you might end up finishing a game in like a half hour of real time, um, at the,

00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:12.520
at this rate. So I, I think it's a pretty good pace.

00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:19.820
Um, I, you know, when I would play sports games in my youth would try and go for realism

00:07:19.820 --> 00:07:25.800
and, uh, that would end up being very long games, especially when like the default is

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:28.500
like a five minute period in hockey or something like that.

00:07:28.500 --> 00:07:37.920
Uh, and that can, that can sometimes like expose how unbalanced the game is because you end

00:07:37.920 --> 00:07:39.400
up scoring like 15 goals.

00:07:39.640 --> 00:07:44.320
If you're, if you're trying to, if you're trying to score a lot, I guess.

00:07:44.460 --> 00:07:46.160
I don't know. It feels all right.

00:07:46.360 --> 00:07:47.700
It depends on the game though too.

00:07:47.840 --> 00:07:53.180
But I, like I was a huge, uh, Wayne Gretzky's 3d hockey fan on the N64.

00:07:53.180 --> 00:08:00.640
And like, if you were to somehow like set that to be an actual, um, you know, hockey period

00:08:00.640 --> 00:08:02.880
length, I don't know what the final score would be.

00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:07.440
Probably be like 99 to 91, you know, like just, just everybody's always scoring in that

00:08:07.440 --> 00:08:08.880
game and it's meant to be fast.

00:08:09.020 --> 00:08:17.560
But I also feel like in, you know, NHL, you know, 25, like it's not the right call to play

00:08:17.560 --> 00:08:18.700
a full match there either.

00:08:18.700 --> 00:08:24.220
Like it's almost never the right call to play the same length as, as an actual game

00:08:24.220 --> 00:08:28.360
is like, even if you're playing MLB, like when you play the full nine innings, it should

00:08:28.360 --> 00:08:32.840
never feel like you committed three hours, uh, of your time.

00:08:33.060 --> 00:08:40.500
That's what I wanted. I wanted 20 minute periods of hockey and I wanted it to feel like, uh, it would like most games

00:08:40.500 --> 00:08:42.120
would end like three, two, four, three.

00:08:42.500 --> 00:08:46.640
And I could never find like the perfect sliders and settings to get there.

00:08:46.640 --> 00:08:51.360
There would always be like nine to seven or I would just get blown out seven to nothing.

00:08:52.220 --> 00:08:56.840
But, uh, I was always searching for like the perfect simulation.

00:08:56.840 --> 00:09:00.240
So I was, I was always trying to, to, to get there.

00:09:00.880 --> 00:09:06.340
Now we can talk about the mechanics of the game and specifically the, I guess the basketball

00:09:06.340 --> 00:09:09.680
part, because there's the, you know, there's the violent part and then there's the basketball

00:09:09.680 --> 00:09:15.680
part. But rather than, you know, talk about the controls or the characters, I just wanted to

00:09:15.680 --> 00:09:21.300
start with that. The way that the game presents itself, as we said, is very, um, it's charming.

00:09:21.480 --> 00:09:28.580
It's silly. It has great presentation between the, uh, the cutaways and, uh, the breaks between, uh,

00:09:28.620 --> 00:09:35.400
the quarters. But the actual like presentation of the basketball, I don't know if you felt this way, but the court

00:09:41.260 --> 00:09:42.080
I don't know.

00:09:42.220 --> 00:09:46.840
This feels very, this feels like NBA jam.

00:09:46.920 --> 00:09:48.280
Like it feels like that long.

00:09:48.280 --> 00:09:52.820
But for some reason, like, I don't know if it's the way that it scrolls, uh, from one

00:09:52.820 --> 00:09:58.980
side to the other, but it created a lot of situations where like, you just don't see the

00:09:58.980 --> 00:10:08.140
other two. And that creates like very confusing, like setups for, for both like to take down the opponents

00:10:08.140 --> 00:10:13.920
and also to like know where like you are in relation to your, uh, you know, if you're

00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:18.180
playing solo with your AI or with your friend, just knowing where you are on screen.

00:10:18.380 --> 00:10:23.300
Like, I just felt like if everything was just kind of condensed to fit on like most of one

00:10:23.300 --> 00:10:26.900
single screen, this game would have been a lot faster.

00:10:26.900 --> 00:10:31.360
You would have scored a lot more and you would have been able to be more aggressive with your,

00:10:31.360 --> 00:10:32.700
with your punches and stuff.

00:10:32.700 --> 00:10:38.040
Because a lot of times, like I just would be hope that I, my punches would connect with

00:10:38.040 --> 00:10:39.260
the, uh, with the opponent.

00:10:39.260 --> 00:10:44.560
And instead the computers mostly were just like, you know, when, once the screen scrolled

00:10:44.560 --> 00:10:47.000
to reveal itself, it was like, oh, I'm left in the dust.

00:10:47.400 --> 00:10:51.640
Uh, yeah, I think there's a balance here because I think that this is as zoomed out as I'd ever

00:10:51.640 --> 00:10:53.860
want a basketball game to be.

00:10:54.080 --> 00:11:00.620
I think if you go any further than you get like just really tiny sprites and the ball would

00:11:00.620 --> 00:11:08.500
be, we would be complaining about how small the ball is or, um, I, I felt that the characters

00:11:08.500 --> 00:11:16.020
off screen were as like, as like difficult to track as in most games that we've played.

00:11:17.220 --> 00:11:23.420
So I thought that it was the right call to, because like, again, you only have to keep

00:11:23.420 --> 00:11:28.220
track of the two and I wasn't doing a lot of like blind passes ahead of me.

00:11:28.440 --> 00:11:31.940
I would usually carry it myself over the, over the half court line.

00:11:32.120 --> 00:11:34.720
And at that point, then you see everybody.

00:11:35.220 --> 00:11:38.220
So maybe it was just my, maybe it was just your, uh, your strategy.

00:11:38.940 --> 00:11:44.900
That's true. Because if you're, if you're being too aggressive, uh, which I guess I was trying to do for a

00:11:44.900 --> 00:11:48.920
large part of the game, like just constantly make sure that my opponent didn't score by

00:11:48.920 --> 00:11:52.600
stealing the ball from them, maybe it would have been better to just be more protective,

00:11:53.220 --> 00:11:57.060
uh, in, in defense and, and hope that they missed their shot to be able to pick up the

00:11:57.060 --> 00:12:02.340
rebound. Because that was another thing that was a little challenging is once, you know, if they make

00:12:02.340 --> 00:12:07.680
an unsuccessful shot, uh, tracking where the ball is for me to pick it up, maybe I'm just

00:12:07.680 --> 00:12:08.920
Oh yeah. Trouble.

00:12:09.340 --> 00:12:11.760
I think there's a bug. I think there's a bug in the game.

00:12:11.880 --> 00:12:13.720
Cause I didn't get a single rebound on my own.

00:12:13.840 --> 00:12:20.360
Like it, I had to just hope that I would be lined up to punch them after the computer

00:12:20.360 --> 00:12:25.740
got the rebound. There's another interesting decision, uh, with the mechanics of the game that like, it

00:12:25.740 --> 00:12:29.760
doesn't matter how far away you are from the net.

00:12:30.340 --> 00:12:34.580
You can take a shot and the ball must touch the net.

00:12:34.780 --> 00:12:36.480
There's no such thing as like air balls.

00:12:36.540 --> 00:12:38.440
Like it won't, it won't go in most likely.

00:12:38.440 --> 00:12:43.700
It probably has like a near 0% chance of going in from, uh, from beyond half court.

00:12:43.740 --> 00:12:48.120
But it's funny that it will just like, it's almost like a glitch to like pass the ball to

00:12:48.120 --> 00:12:53.760
your side of the, uh, the, uh, court to make sure that the opponent doesn't like quickly

00:12:54.260 --> 00:12:56.660
steal it and then take another shot.

00:12:56.900 --> 00:13:02.000
It's definitely a cheese tactic that I was doing when I could tell that they were coming

00:13:02.000 --> 00:13:05.700
at me for like, I just inbounded and they're already pushing up.

00:13:05.800 --> 00:13:10.040
I'm like, I'm just going to make sure you don't get this or I'm going to delay you by

00:13:10.040 --> 00:13:12.800
five seconds to have to drive up the full court again.

00:13:12.800 --> 00:13:19.200
Um, but yeah, I, I did notice that a lot of it had more to do with your distance from

00:13:19.200 --> 00:13:21.220
another, like it's even outlined.

00:13:21.220 --> 00:13:30.520
Like there is kind of like a mini instruction booklet in the game itself, uh, that like how

00:13:30.520 --> 00:13:37.520
far away you are from, uh, your opponent, like will determine the pressure on you, which

00:13:37.520 --> 00:13:40.060
has some effect on your accuracy.

00:13:40.060 --> 00:13:45.120
But I feel like there was more focus on that than there was like, this is from just beyond

00:13:45.120 --> 00:13:47.860
the three point line to this is at half court.

00:13:48.420 --> 00:13:56.840
Yeah. So like, was it mostly, you know, for me, NBA jam is mostly a game where like, when I have

00:13:56.840 --> 00:14:02.140
the ball, I just try and like, you know, rush it and, uh, get a fast break and then just

00:14:02.140 --> 00:14:04.080
like, you know, do a cool dunk and score.

00:14:04.280 --> 00:14:06.900
And then like, hopefully, you know, regain possession.

00:14:06.900 --> 00:14:13.760
Is this more of a offensive game than a defensive game or do real players know that it's a balance

00:14:13.760 --> 00:14:16.060
and you, and you have to kind of play both sides?

00:14:16.800 --> 00:14:21.780
Well, it's definitely more aggressive game because I think in most basketball games, I

00:14:21.780 --> 00:14:24.400
don't even know what the steel button is really doing.

00:14:24.400 --> 00:14:30.940
Like between the animation of just like some weird little, like, like spastic move.

00:14:31.100 --> 00:14:37.280
And I'd never know the positioning you're supposed to have during a steel.

00:14:37.980 --> 00:14:46.440
Um, so I, I was always very confused when playing defense on these like NES basketball games.

00:14:46.440 --> 00:14:48.400
Whereas with this, it's a lot simpler.

00:14:48.580 --> 00:14:50.000
Like you just go up and punch the guy.

00:14:50.220 --> 00:14:57.060
And usually if you're on the same, like, I guess like Z axis and facing the right direction,

00:14:57.060 --> 00:14:58.920
he's going to get punched.

00:14:58.920 --> 00:15:05.660
And it feels very satisfying when you punch the guy and, uh, and he does drop the ball.

00:15:05.840 --> 00:15:12.380
So I, I wouldn't say that it's like, uh, a more defensive game, but I would say it's a much more aggressive game.

00:15:12.380 --> 00:15:19.320
Um, like whether you're playing defense or offense, you do have to kind of do what you were doing and just get up in there,

00:15:19.340 --> 00:15:22.560
whether you're just going to punch or if you're going to try and tackle them.

00:15:23.120 --> 00:15:29.480
So tackling, I, I could never tell when a tackle actually was better than a punch.

00:15:29.600 --> 00:15:32.840
Maybe if you're chasing them or something, but yeah, maybe if you're like from behind.

00:15:33.200 --> 00:15:41.780
Yeah. Um, but I, I think that it's, you definitely need to get your shots off quick because the computer will

00:15:41.780 --> 00:15:42.740
punch you.

00:15:44.140 --> 00:15:46.080
Yeah. Definitely more offensive focus.

00:15:46.580 --> 00:15:47.520
Yeah. It's funny.

00:15:47.600 --> 00:15:53.100
The manual mentions, uh, in the tips from the coach section, uh, follow the control movements

00:15:53.100 --> 00:15:56.820
given to you in, in these instructions, then experiment on your own.

00:15:56.940 --> 00:16:00.140
You might surprise yourself and your opponent with what you come up with.

00:16:00.140 --> 00:16:03.620
It's all in hitting the right combination of buttons at the right time.

00:16:03.640 --> 00:16:07.140
That makes it seem that there's like mortal combat level fatalities.

00:16:07.140 --> 00:16:10.080
Yeah. If you, but it, but it's not really that right.

00:16:10.080 --> 00:16:12.380
Like I never saw any like fun animations.

00:16:12.380 --> 00:16:18.380
If I like the tackle is kind of like a lunge and that's silly in itself.

00:16:18.380 --> 00:16:23.460
But like, I didn't see any like creative animations other than punches and tackles.

00:16:23.460 --> 00:16:28.480
No, I, I, I guess there's a little bit of context based like adjustments.

00:16:28.500 --> 00:16:36.300
Like if you completely whiff you'll, uh, your tackle, you'll just sort of keep rolling, which

00:16:36.300 --> 00:16:37.360
I guess is cool.

00:16:37.360 --> 00:16:46.520
And depending on where you take a shot, um, you'll either do a jump shot or a not so flagrant,

00:16:47.580 --> 00:16:56.000
um, dunk, uh, but, and then sometimes you'll do a really weird, like princess peach in Mario,

00:16:56.240 --> 00:17:04.900
Mario party, super Mario, uh, two, uh, where you'll just jump to the rim height and then

00:17:04.900 --> 00:17:08.460
very slowly get closer to the rim to dunk.

00:17:08.640 --> 00:17:14.340
Uh, which I thought was pretty funny, but I don't think any of those have like actual effects

00:17:14.340 --> 00:17:15.520
on the game.

00:17:15.740 --> 00:17:17.000
It's just different animations.

00:17:18.000 --> 00:17:19.820
Yeah. Which maybe is what they were implying.

00:17:20.060 --> 00:17:26.380
Um, in the arcade version, uh, doing like a, a, a lunge towards the opponent could trigger

